#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091900 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "4a734f55-ceac-587c-a831-fa8bed578a99" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-148" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y = 2x^3 -x^2$ at the point $(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x^3-x^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nf'(x) =6x^2 -2x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$:\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = 6(1)^2 - 2(1) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac 1 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091901 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "df59a07a-922c-50b9-8c2c-22ed57ac2bda" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-149" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x} -4x$ at the point $(1,-2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2\\sqrt{x} - 4x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{1/2} - 4x) \\\\\n&= x^{-1/2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = (1)^{-1/2} - 4 = -3\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = \\dfrac 1 3\n$$\n\nThe equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - (-2) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 1) \\\\\ny + 2 &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 1) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{1}{3} - 2 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}x &= \\frac{7}{3} \\\\\nx &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(7,0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092030 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "e405bf60-d500-55df-90be-a938dc9dfc93" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-6080" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^3 - x^2$ at the point $(1,0)$ is given by $y=px+q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the values of $p$ and $q$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^3 - x^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nf^{′}(x) = \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3} - x^{2}) = 3x^{2} - 2x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1, 0){:}$\n\n$$\nm = f^{′}(1) = 3 - 2 = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=-1\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 0 &= - (x - 1) \\\\\ny &= -x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d8e7f13-0128-5f62-8243-068e418dc0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247069} {:answer/id #uuid "d4859282-70e0-552b-8828-e43fcc39022e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247070} {:answer/id #uuid "0d5d904e-1610-5676-a787-b4cb2f9a64cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247071} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd7f921-42e4-5cf8-9ba1-e20b085904db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247072} {:answer/id #uuid "4aee79a0-82f0-5a57-9fd9-50245c95cb14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dd7f921-42e4-5cf8-9ba1-e20b085904db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247072} :answer-field/id #uuid "435d08fe-81af-56cb-a2e7-b26b52c4ed70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247068}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a59feec-a5b0-5815-a6e4-8461a44411c8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103531" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-2{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-2{x}^{2}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3/2}-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{1/2}-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{1/2}-4(1)=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b1dc0a6-b157-5ec3-9a98-2f62414ed8fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247076} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfde0b1-c906-5337-a0df-0c668a082fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247077} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa7f4ee-559f-584e-9a21-c244d64f3be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247078} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8cbafe-c47f-531b-9892-cd90a1e83ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247079} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fbef3f-830a-5b02-8671-4ce91a4ecca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd8cbafe-c47f-531b-9892-cd90a1e83ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247079} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e96678-5941-5ce3-a7ef-2e1c69ddc50d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247075}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2802d9a2-5381-5500-9334-76d44ddd233b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103532" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{3}-4\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}$ at the point $(1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-4\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-4\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{2}-10(1)^{3/2}=-7\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line  ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e178bc89-6160-5d80-9199-00128cc67c6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247083} {:answer/id #uuid "827b0b85-5010-5ec0-9df5-40aa969e0cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247084} {:answer/id #uuid "d88c7d1b-cb8d-567d-976d-efddd791b3c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247085} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ac7ca8-1fd9-5de8-a7a1-4c83377e4054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247086} {:answer/id #uuid "9a93fd74-afd2-51cb-8de2-d06a2803f895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a93fd74-afd2-51cb-8de2-d06a2803f895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247087} :answer-field/id #uuid "725c399c-fd74-52ab-9a33-120f938060b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247082}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9053d83-88f1-5abe-83c4-b5466b79614e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103543" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x$ at the point $(1,9).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-5}+7x) \\\\  & =-10{x}^{-6}+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=-10(1)^{-6}+7=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc92ea6c-546e-5c02-a31d-5db71be92a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247090} {:answer/id #uuid "08a71dde-9ffd-5cec-a32f-702f585847b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247091} {:answer/id #uuid "ca569003-7200-53dc-becb-c5c0ef6958ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247092} {:answer/id #uuid "cb40ced7-1bc1-5fd8-943b-3f837416c6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247093} {:answer/id #uuid "246671cb-963f-50f5-aafa-ae05eb3150cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246671cb-963f-50f5-aafa-ae05eb3150cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247094} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d6edde-8082-5afb-b4b4-7f44dd8e85fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247089}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f2145a2-4a3b-5f81-b58c-58ecc05ee736" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-104073" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(8,12)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(8)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-12 & =-(x-8) \\\\ y & =-x+8+12 \\\\ y & =20-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20-0 \\\\ y & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,20).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bd66a81-6297-5294-b79c-358bf0517640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{7},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247097} {:answer/id #uuid "65d2585c-2e1f-5c4d-b035-23d4271d7fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247098} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9e61e3-3099-5473-8df7-019bdaca9185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247099} {:answer/id #uuid "94ef35e8-94e7-53c3-836b-7bf2ed2fa78a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247100} {:answer/id #uuid "54769853-ac06-5717-bda6-aab66c905c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94ef35e8-94e7-53c3-836b-7bf2ed2fa78a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247100} :answer-field/id #uuid "90bd12a9-c2c6-5f43-b685-9095e24157fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247096}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d576f2ce-7e11-5d35-84cc-2026c401aedc" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-104075" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=3\\sqrt{1}=3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at  $(7,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae589b44-4a4d-5aab-b06e-b155ce97dd52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089179} {:answer/id #uuid "c77360b4-5594-5ff2-94e2-529915aa5f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089180} {:answer/id #uuid "e175c968-e6fc-5732-8583-17cb60b4c41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089181} {:answer/id #uuid "1efadb9e-930f-5f85-9638-cdc930a4d33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089182} {:answer/id #uuid "548169f7-a15a-505e-ac9b-b1366d2cb71d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e175c968-e6fc-5732-8583-17cb60b4c41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089181} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cd5ef3b-90b7-58f5-83b5-f471bd111162" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089178}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24c7ef87-44ff-5aaa-8c80-717e24087d16" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-11124" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(1, 2)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+2 \\\\ y & =-x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0+3 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b97c6a8a-e396-5c3c-9d0e-91d4f8962d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b97c6a8a-e396-5c3c-9d0e-91d4f8962d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247104} :answer-field/id #uuid "d929ed43-918c-51fe-82f0-6fa7fa13ddbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247103} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc7a2d36-09f8-5815-9bfb-b435098c4258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc7a2d36-09f8-5815-9bfb-b435098c4258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247106} :answer-field/id #uuid "5da05495-4397-56ff-8ebc-6eef5a42d5b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247105}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1eef92d-2d17-5de7-8bd9-e36057891904" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342162" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=6{x}^{2}-7x+4$ at the point $(0,4).$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-7x+4.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-7x+4)=12x-7\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,4):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(0)=12(0)-7=-7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{7}(x-0) \\\\ 7(y-4) & =x \\\\ 7y-28 & =x \\\\ x-7\\cdot y & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b84b4e3a-a5df-5496-9792-aa71771c5f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b84b4e3a-a5df-5496-9792-aa71771c5f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247109} :answer-field/id #uuid "24355355-17a0-5ccb-8a06-77025f490d86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247108} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96898260-8991-548c-a847-706338ff8c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96898260-8991-548c-a847-706338ff8c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247111} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d939833-5b0a-593c-bf25-f9df66d9a5e3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247110}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7264db2-9dde-5fdd-9dff-7c1e888caf28" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342168" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,-6).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3})=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}-\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-6):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}({2}^{5})-\\frac{21}{4}({2}^{2})=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ -3(y+6) & =x-2 \\\\ -3y-18 & =x-2 \\\\ x+3\\cdot y & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71282172-bb03-581c-b587-7167173bb57d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247114} {:answer/id #uuid "38c9d6ba-8068-5a75-88fb-0ce10dfaa9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71282172-bb03-581c-b587-7167173bb57d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247114} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec52a40-e577-5cf1-9f3c-92667e03e3e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a847893-ffea-5286-9e8c-81af2273cfa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a847893-ffea-5286-9e8c-81af2273cfa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247117} :answer-field/id #uuid "44983d4e-a00f-5410-bf21-87280d8b73f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247116}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d801a557-bb36-50c1-8317-3d5072ae1f0f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342190" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$8x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{8})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}}}=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{3}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16(y-\\frac{3}{4}) & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8x+1 \\\\ 8x+16\\cdot y & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db0cd71a-7e9b-5a54-b88a-373fc76fe3fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db0cd71a-7e9b-5a54-b88a-373fc76fe3fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247120} :answer-field/id #uuid "b01d0d0d-74d7-5993-9ebb-998c70c1883f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5b1ba38-d11d-59bb-a8d6-46a5af09d418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5b1ba38-d11d-59bb-a8d6-46a5af09d418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247122} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9462681-2595-5b9d-b3a5-3c3bd6ad79e0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247121}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d547e1c1-a0cb-5242-95b6-cf10768bcc9d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342200" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{2})=-2x.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=-2(1)=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "586d4cf0-32d5-5f1f-b575-671f00d3e24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247125} {:answer/id #uuid "450d4247-2193-5884-bc24-36fc9af212a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586d4cf0-32d5-5f1f-b575-671f00d3e24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247125} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b119a5a-7499-566f-8ad8-8fd4eb81017f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "883a065a-4ea0-5c8d-a890-a2aa7d8a164f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "883a065a-4ea0-5c8d-a890-a2aa7d8a164f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247128} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ac3d034-56e1-5503-8551-fc8dd1726b22" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247127}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "871045b9-7122-52ce-88ea-7b0a5214a2f2" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342201" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}-6x+5$ at the point $(0,5)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+5)=6x-6.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(0)=6(0)-6=-6,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-6)}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x-0) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}x+5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,-30,0\\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bfcbb8f-614b-5429-8f72-a29b3fe17079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247131} {:answer/id #uuid "3f513769-7378-5e17-b62b-1aa250e69edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247132} {:answer/id #uuid "b7573ece-07fe-55c4-8347-06a98b4db947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247133} {:answer/id #uuid "b179bd3e-21e5-54ef-ba08-f8aea0013cb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247134} {:answer/id #uuid "1632a71f-b967-5bd3-8c73-eb9fe071dcd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7573ece-07fe-55c4-8347-06a98b4db947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247133} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb59007-aa21-5275-9ba0-7da05c2ab3f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247130}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97b2d267-56ec-5ab2-9366-02087bc45c1d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-47409" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-x$ at the point $(1,3)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p-q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-2}-x) \\\\  & =-8{x}^{-3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-8(1)^{-3}-1=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-1) \\\\ 9y-27 & =x-1 \\\\ x-9y & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p-q=-9-(-26)=17.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64452545-66ae-51c8-a67c-2a416bfb7390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247138} {:answer/id #uuid "08519b3a-97b6-5245-9ed9-75842ee68711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247139} {:answer/id #uuid "90ea3c86-7a25-529e-b33f-8888f0fe7f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247140} {:answer/id #uuid "51c29d6b-73b4-5613-9ef2-b1c56d654864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247141} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdc2057-b94f-5b06-8234-175b6c3c8acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51c29d6b-73b4-5613-9ef2-b1c56d654864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247141} :answer-field/id #uuid "260aa085-bdb1-509e-a7c9-6c6f8bf8a9da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247137}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "414ee72f-747c-573a-9cad-a9cbdad6101a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-47410" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+4x$ at the point $(1,7)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+4x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+4x)=6x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=6(1)+4=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(7) & =-\\frac{1}{10}(x-1) \\\\ 10y-70 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+10y & =71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=10+71=81.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55d8746b-62c1-5152-952d-ff53dce159a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247145} {:answer/id #uuid "225a4b80-a8db-52bc-948a-a3222ab8f232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247146} {:answer/id #uuid "146026ae-067b-5834-aed2-bf73abae8439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247147} {:answer/id #uuid "049b1a4a-857d-51a7-a12b-33c3183a63b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247148} {:answer/id #uuid "a63802d9-071d-5d7d-bcc0-ac9255380df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "225a4b80-a8db-52bc-948a-a3222ab8f232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247146} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f304064-9fb5-5997-9fd0-8426b2ba82d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247144}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cc3fcbf-282d-56d9-bd28-ab237fb3be15" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-47449" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=2(1)+1=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152a5ce0-d99c-5704-a2bf-76fceb4e4d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247152} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad2b576-1dc5-5a5b-be5f-2503066ec7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247153} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8b56cc-2466-5452-978e-80583b4d7ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247154} {:answer/id #uuid "28137cd4-79da-54b7-b653-5c7d412fcbcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247155} {:answer/id #uuid "666f0a9c-3681-5c0b-b4f6-8cf9c37f580f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28137cd4-79da-54b7-b653-5c7d412fcbcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247155} :answer-field/id #uuid "54f76137-dccf-5c0c-95dd-e907db765f70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247151}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a12a2764-b91f-5fe7-ab73-4b3777d499eb" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-71524" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}-5$ at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-5.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-5)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(2)=2(2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}]}
